Z||000003||Patrick||11-11-1999||11:32 PM||||<BR>Well, part of the problem may have to do with daylight savings time. <P>I guess Cliff is responsible for the other 12 minute discrepancy.<P>It's almost impossible to find a good webmaster these days... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000004||theogre||11-12-1999||01:31 AM||||Ohhh.... Time..... There's a raw nerve.<P>Cliff recently reset his server for EDT. Now we are on EDT. So there's your hour. Show me a PC that doesn't have a time drift,,,,,, there's your other few minutes. <P>Keep in mind that the server is in the Netherlands. I don't think Cliff has daylight time. So he's probly forgotten about it.<P>Does it really matter? Unless you are regulaly setting against USNO, NIST, or some other GPS based clock most machines are drifting farther than you would think. Most machines on a network <B>can</B> get their time from a server, but this only happens at login in most cases, and in MSWinNT logins only when the admin has properly configured a login script. <P>Machines that are on all the time drift like blazes unless they are synced to a GPS or other "atomic grade" clock. I've had file servers that drift 20-30 minutes a month. This is why NTP and Netware NDS time sync exist. You get one clock you know is good and stable, and skip the hardware clock in the local machines. <P>The local clocks often use uncompensated and uncalibrated "watch" crystals as a time base. Even very expensive machines use these lame clocks. To make it worse, many Wintel machines are so overloaded the OS can't keep proper time either due to missed interupts and other things...<P>The number of machines keeping accurate time without help is very small. I use a version of "socket watch" myself to keep my server and main workstation close to a nist clock. ((At the current check I'm only a couple second off..... I've been off by several hundred.))||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||11-12-1999||03:58 AM||||Acually, I had forgotten to set the time to daylight savings time - good thing you reminded me! Clock should now be correct. Maybe I should install one of those programs that sets the time periodically using Frankfurt's atom clock. I've got a similar program running on my machine at home and that PC is never more than a few seconds off.<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000006||DJRice||11-12-1999||09:15 AM||||Our admin network at work is pretty cool because it gets its time from GPS.  I can type "Clock" in my location bar in netscape and view the GPS clock, and also get info on what satellites and transponders are available at the time.  ||||reg||1
